          "pseudo_id": "p1",
          "pseudo": "It all began with a single, almost invisible click in a database. When the casting team for Christopher Nolan's 'The Odyssey' was finalizing their shortlist, an algorithm sorting through thousands of headshots experienced a fractional-second lag. This delay, caused by a background software update running on the casting director's laptop, meant the sequence in which the names were presented was imperceptibly shuffled. As a result, Lupita Nyong'o's profile, which was always on the list, appeared at the top of the 'Helen of Troy' sub-folder at the exact moment the director glanced at his screen for a final review. That trivial visual nudge, a product of a millisecond of network jitter, solidified the choice.",
          "source": {
            "agent_id": "A7",
